<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles II of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1332">1332</a>–<a href="/years/1387">1387</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Charles the Bad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles II, called the Bad, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from 1349 and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>, a grandson of Louis X of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through his mother Joan II. Holding large lands in Normandy, he intrigued against John II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> of France, switching between the English and French during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, and had the constable Charles de la Cerda murdered in 1354. Charles V's armies seized most of his Norman lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Charles II of Navarre" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_II_of_Navarre.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> on 10 October 1332, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-navarre">Philip III of Navarre</a>, count of Évreux, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-ii-of-navarre">Joan II of Navarre</a>, the only daughter of Louis X of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His mother had received extensive lands in Normandy as compensation for resigning her claims to France, Champagne and Brie in 1328, so that Charles held Évreux, Mortain, parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vexin">Vexin</a> and a portion of the Cotentin as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. He was raised in France and was declared king at 17. He spent most of his reign there, regarding Navarre chiefly as a source of manpower for his designs on the French throne, to which he hoped for recognition as heir-general of Philip IV through his mother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1352 Charles married Joan of Valois, daughter of King John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Jealous of the Constable of France, Charles de la Cerda, he arranged his murder at l'Aigle on 8 January 1354 and made no secret of it. He then intrigued with the English, which led John II to make peace in the Treaty of Mantes on 22 February 1354 on terms that enlarged Charles's possessions. This became his pattern: he used the threat of an English alliance to wring concessions from the French king, as again in the Treaty of Valognes of 10 September 1355.</p><p>After John II was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, Charles was freed from prison on 9 November 1357 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to a hero's welcome. He allied with Étienne Marcel against the Dauphin, but in 1358 he led the suppression of the Jacquerie at the Battle of Mello on 10 June. His bid to become Captain of Paris failed, and on 19 August 1359 he renounced his demands. In 1361 he claimed the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, which John II took instead. When war resumed in 1369 he again offered alliances to both Charles V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, but paid homage to Charles V in March 1371.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> on 1 January 1387, aged 54. His death was known all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and was often cited by moralists. Versions of the story vary in detail. In Francis Blagdon's account of 1803, a physician had him wrapped in linen soaked in brandy, and a servant sewing the cloth closed used a candle to burn off the thread; the cloth caught fire and he was burned alive. His children included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-navarre">Charles III of Navarre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles II of Navarre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Hundred Years' War, a French army under Bertrand du Guesclin defeated the Anglo-Navarrese army of King <b>Charles II of Navarre</b>, called the Bad, at Cocherel.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-16">16 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles the Bad</b> of Navarre used Évreux as a base for his intrigues against John II and Charles V, who seized most of his Norman lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-evreux">County of Évreux</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Navarreria, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.02,1.15&amp;year=1367" aria-label="Évreux on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4577 453 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles II of Navarre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5027 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5027 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.02,1.15&amp;year=1367">What was here in 1367? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
